        "bodyTr": "Today's young people are caught in an unprecedented \"employment ice age.\" Last month, the number of employed youth aged 15 to 29 decreased by 196,000 compared to a year ago. This marks the 48th consecutive month of decline. The youth employment rate of 42.9 percent has also been falling for 30 consecutive months. As companies increasingly prefer experienced workers and adopt continuous recruitment practices, youth employment is becoming ever more difficult. Despite this dire youth employment situation, the labor community is turning a blind eye to the tears of young people struggling in the job market. Yesterday, the Democratic Party's Special Committee on Mandatory Retirement Age resumed labor-management consultations for the first time in 10 months, but ultimately failed to narrow their differences. The special committee decided to hold its final plenary session on the 2nd of next month and proceed with legislation within the year regardless of whether labor and management reach an agreement.\n\nThe mediation proposal prepared by the Democratic Party committee raises the statutory retirement age to 61 in 2029, then increases it by one year every two years to reach 65 by 2037. The proposal also includes mandatory \"re-employment\" provisions allowing companies to rehire retiring employees from 2028 to 2036 to work for an additional year. Both labor and management are opposing this mediation proposal. The labor community is maintaining its existing position on a comprehensive extension of the retirement age and even mentions the possibility of breaking policy cooperation with the government if legislation fails to pass this year. The business community argues that employment continuation systems should be designed around post-retirement re-employment rather than a broad extension, citing concerns about increased labor costs and potential contraction in youth hiring.\n\nGiven the trend of low birth rates and an aging population, there are inevitable reasons for extending the retirement age. As the working-age population shrinks, labor supply also becomes scarce. When older workers remain employed longer, it benefits not only themselves but the entire national economy. Above all, there is a need to bridge the \"income crevasse\" between the statutory retirement age and when people begin receiving national pensions. However, in Korea's reality of high wage seniority and rigid employment structures, a blanket extension of the retirement age risks exacerbating structural problems in the labor market. The wage level of Korean workers with 30 or more years of service is 2.95 times that of workers with less than one year of service, higher than the European Union average of 1.65 times or Japan's 2.27 times. Companies will ultimately reduce youth hiring due to increased labor costs. The 2016 retirement age extension, which uniformly raised the retirement age to 60, demonstrated this exact pattern. The benefits of that retirement age extension concentrated on unionized and large company jobs with relatively stronger employment protections. According to the Bank of Korea, for every one older worker added due to that retirement age extension, youth employment decreased by 0.4 to 1.5 workers. We should reference Japan's experience, which designed the system gradually based on social consensus and substantially extended the retirement age. Japan has maintained a statutory retirement age of 60 for 28 years since 1998. However, instead of legal mandates, it pursued policies encouraging companies' voluntary retirement age extensions. Companies were given the choice of re-employment, retirement age extension, or abolishing mandatory retirement. The labor community accepted lower wage levels in re-employment compared to pre-retirement peak wages. The business community progressively employed those who wished to continue working until age 65, reformed wage systems, and implemented career development programs. The government partially subsidized wage reductions for re-employed workers. Labor, management, and government shared social costs related to extending employment for older workers in a balanced manner.\n\nThe government and ruling party pressure for retirement age extension while failing to discuss reforming high-wage seniority practices and making the employment market more flexible. Retirement age extension without labor market flexibility will ultimately increase corporate burdens and lead to reduced new hiring. This would be unfortunate for young people and would darken Korea's economic future. The government and ruling party must not allow themselves to be swept along by the demands of their labor supporter base and proceed with a blanket retirement age extension while ignoring the desperate circumstances of young people.",
        "perspective": "청년 희생 경고음",
